WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:14.396
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:14.776 --> 00:00:21.259
Welcome back to Superstars in History, the talk show interviewing famous people from the past.

3
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:30.324
I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest was an inventor who really lit up the world!

4
00:00:30.952 --> 00:00:39.442
He held 1,093 patents for his inventions, but you probably know him for his most famous creation, the

5
00:00:39.502 --> 00:00:44.087
lightbulb. Please welcome the very bright Thomas Edison!

6
00:00:44.107 --> 00:01:01.221
THOMAS EDISON: Thank you, Faye.

7
00:01:01.701 --> 00:01:03.062
I'm excited to be here.

8
00:01:03.082 --> 00:01:09.427
FAYE MOSS: You were born the youngest of seven children in 1847, in Ohio.

9
00:01:09.947 --> 00:01:13.209
Did your genius shine through as a child?

10
00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:18.113
THOMAS EDISON: Oh, no, I was a pretty poor student in school.

11
00:01:19.013 --> 00:01:25.118
My teachers thought my mind wandered too much, which caused my mother to teach me at home instead.

12
00:01:25.998 --> 00:01:29.989
My mother was one of my biggest supporters; she always believed in me.

13
00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:33.631
FAYE MOSS: She must have been a brilliant teacher!

14
00:01:33.651 --> 00:01:40.355
THOMAS EDISON: My mother actually was a schoolteacher, but I stopped my education when I was 12 to

15
00:01:40.375 --> 00:01:43.857
get a job selling newspapers and candy on a train.

16
00:01:44.798 --> 00:01:46.559
The job gave me lots of free time.

17
00:01:47.039 --> 00:01:51.842
Oh, and by then, I was losing my hearing and could only hear a little out of one

18
00:01:52.002 --> 00:01:57.897
ear. I'm not sure what caused it, but I didn't let it interfere with any of my other

19
00:01:58.057 --> 00:02:05.739
interests. For example, in the train's baggage car, I had a printing press, which I used to start

20
00:02:05.779 --> 00:02:06.899
my own newspaper.

21
00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:11.100
I even set up a laboratory for my chemistry experiments.

22
00:02:11.881 --> 00:02:16.362
But one day a bottle of phosphorous fell and set the baggage car on fire.

23
00:02:17.382 --> 00:02:21.043
The conductor was so mad at me, he threw me off the train!

24
00:02:21.063 --> 00:02:24.444
FAYE MOSS: That wasn't your last train ride though.

25
00:02:25.256 --> 00:02:31.378
In fact, a near accident with a train led to your working with the telegraph.

26
00:02:31.498 --> 00:02:33.618
THOMAS EDISON: Yes, that's true!

27
00:02:33.718 --> 00:02:38.600
In 1862 I rescued a three-year-old from a railroad track when a train was about to hit him.

28
00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:45.342
As a reward, the boy's father taught me all about railroad telegraphs and Morse code.

29
00:02:46.242 --> 00:02:49.803
I was able to use those skills to get work as a telegraph operator.

30
00:02:50.663 --> 00:02:53.084
This work let me travel around the U.S.

31
00:02:54.104 --> 00:03:01.229
But I always knew what I really wanted to do was invent things, so I started inventing on

32
00:03:01.269 --> 00:03:02.009
the side.

33
00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:05.892
FAYE MOSS: What was your first major invention?

34
00:03:05.912 --> 00:03:13.337
THOMAS EDISON: In June 1869 I received my very first patent for an electric vote recorder.

35
00:03:14.298 --> 00:03:20.922
Even though politicians didn't want to use my machine, I wasn't too discouraged, at least not at first.

36
00:03:21.823 --> 00:03:24.753
Inventing was fun—maybe too much fun!

37
00:03:25.753 --> 00:03:30.255
In 1871 I married my first wife, Mary Stilwell.

38
00:03:31.136 --> 00:03:37.239
We had three children, two of whom I nicknamed Dot and Dash after the telegraphic terms.

39
00:03:38.379 --> 00:03:44.182
Mary was often ill and unhappy about how much time I spent on my inventions.

40
00:03:45.122 --> 00:03:48.084
She needed me to devote more hours to taking care of her.

41
00:03:49.492 --> 00:03:55.457
Eventually I decided that I wouldn't waste time inventing things that no one wanted.

42
00:03:55.477 --> 00:03:57.939
FAYE MOSS: What did people want?

43
00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:00.281
THOMAS EDISON: A better telegraph!

44
00:04:01.142 --> 00:04:07.827
Like some other impressive inventors at the time, including Alexander Graham Bell, I thought I could make the

45
00:04:07.867 --> 00:04:09.549
machine faster and better.

46
00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:11.210
And in 1874 I had a breakthrough!

47
00:04:14.248 --> 00:04:21.270
My quadruplex telegraph let people send four signals on a single wire at the same time.

48
00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:28.272
My invention was so successful, I was able to buy a new laboratory in Menlo Park, New Jersey.

49
00:04:29.072 --> 00:04:31.633
It became known as the Invention Factory.

50
00:04:31.653 --> 00:04:37.355
FAYE MOSS: You and Alexander Graham Bell seem to have had similar interests.

51
00:04:37.375 --> 00:04:43.757
THOMAS EDISON: I always felt like I was competing with him, since we both wanted to improve sound

52
00:04:43.877 --> 00:04:44.918
and communication.

53
00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:51.460
When he said he'd invented a device to transmit the human voice, I wanted to make an even

54
00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:52.261
better one!

55
00:04:53.281 --> 00:05:00.404
I ended up creating a transmitter that let people use the telephone over much greater distances than Bell's

56
00:05:00.464 --> 00:05:01.545
original design.

57
00:05:01.565 --> 00:05:06.407
FAYE MOSS: The telephone also inspired another invention of yours, didn't it?

58
00:05:06.427 --> 00:05:08.308
THOMAS EDISON: Yes!

59
00:05:08.996 --> 00:05:15.441
I wanted to record telephone messages mechanically, so I came up with the phonograph!

60
00:05:16.442 --> 00:05:24.147
When sound traveled through the machine, the vibrations caused a needle to make indentations on a cylinder.

61
00:05:25.308 --> 00:05:27.810
Then the cylinder could play back a recording.

62
00:05:30.251 --> 00:05:37.332
To test the device, I spoke the words "Mary had a little lamb" into the mouthpiece, and it

63
00:05:37.372 --> 00:05:38.552
played the phrase back to me.

64
00:05:42.574 --> 00:05:46.835
Newspapers and critics were amazed, and I became quite famous.

65
00:05:47.715 --> 00:05:51.676
Some even called me the Wizard of Menlo Park after that.

66
00:05:51.716 --> 00:05:56.698
FAYE MOSS: The phonograph wasn't your last act of wizardry.

67
00:05:57.498 --> 00:06:00.379
Tell us about inventing the lightbulb!

68
00:06:01.059 --> 00:06:05.444
THOMAS EDISON: I actually wasn't the first to make a lightbulb.

69
00:06:05.464 --> 00:06:10.028
However, most of the lightbulbs that had been created only stayed lit for a few minutes.

70
00:06:11.048 --> 00:06:14.851
Or they were too expensive to be widely used.

71
00:06:14.911 --> 00:06:21.335
In 1878 I made my own lightbulb with help from a few friends, and I worked on perfecting

72
00:06:21.395 --> 00:06:27.780
it. By the next year, I had made one that lasted 13.5 hours.

73
00:06:30.182 --> 00:06:35.553
I went on to create bulbs that lasted even longer—up to 1,200 hours!

74
00:06:35.573 --> 00:06:43.017
FAYE MOSS: So if the lightbulb already existed, why did you get so much credit for it?

75
00:06:43.037 --> 00:06:50.782
THOMAS EDISON: Well, mine was the first that was both efficient and affordable, which allowed electric lightbulbs to

76
00:06:50.802 --> 00:06:51.963
be widely used.

77
00:06:52.943 --> 00:06:59.727
Thanks to my invention, the first lighting system that could support a whole city was installed in New

78
00:06:59.787 --> 00:07:00.388
York City.

79
00:07:01.436 --> 00:07:06.681
Soon cities across the world, like Paris, London, and Moscow, lit up!

80
00:07:09.143 --> 00:07:16.429
I also had my own electric company, which attracted young, talented inventors and entrepreneurs such as Nikola Tesla

81
00:07:16.649 --> 00:07:17.569
and Henry Ford.

82
00:07:17.589 --> 00:07:21.413
FAYE MOSS: Did you have any other popular inventions?

83
00:07:21.433 --> 00:07:27.858
THOMAS EDISON: I went on to invent many other things, but not all were successful.

84
00:07:28.939 --> 00:07:33.722
One of my favorites was a motion picture device I worked on in the 1890s.

85
00:07:34.102 --> 00:07:38.164
This device was called the Kinetoscope.

86
00:07:39.165 --> 00:07:41.246
Places opened in major U.S.

87
00:07:41.286 --> 00:07:46.108
cities so people could watch silent, very short films on the machines.

88
00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:55.153
Eventually one of my assistants and I were able to create another device—one that could project pictures and

89
00:07:55.273 --> 00:07:56.074
make sound!

90
00:07:56.094 --> 00:08:03.651
FAYE MOSS: Your phonograph, lightbulb, and Kinetoscope definitely had a big influence on the world.

91
00:08:04.652 --> 00:08:07.855
Thanks so much for sharing your genius with us today!

92
00:08:07.875 --> 00:08:14.601
THOMAS EDISON: Genius is 1 percent inspiration and 99 percent hard work!

93
00:08:14.841 --> 00:08:22.128
Good-bye! FAYE MOSS: Thomas Edison never lost his passion for inventing.

94
00:08:23.089 --> 00:08:29.234
He developed processes to build houses with cement and did research and experiments for the U.S.

95
00:08:29.274 --> 00:08:30.915
Navy during World War I.

96
00:08:31.816 --> 00:08:35.960
Even in his seventies, he continued to work and stay active.

97
00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:40.703
He died on October 18, 1931.

98
00:08:41.644 --> 00:08:48.830
President Hoover asked everyone to turn off all electric lights across the country on the evening of Edison's

99
00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.192
funeral to remember his accomplishments.

100
00:08:52.168 --> 00:08:58.113
Edison's inventions certainly lit up the world and made our lives a lot brighter.

101
00:08:58.133 --> 00:09:00.876
Thanks, everyone, for watching today.

102
00:09:00.896 --> 00:09:06.421
I'll be back again soon with another famous guest from history!
